AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms and their application to disease detection and decision support for healthcare professions have greatly evolved in the recent decade. AI has been widely applied and explored in gastroenterology for endoscopic analysis to diagnose intestinal cancers, premalignant polyps, gastrointestinal inflammatory lesions, and bleeding. Patients' responses to treatments and prognoses have both been predicted using AI by combining multiple algorithms. In this review, we explored the recent applications of AI algorithms in the identification and characterization of intestinal polyps and colorectal cancer predictions. AI-based pre-diction models have the potential to help medical practitioners diagnose, establish prognoses, and find accurate conclusions for the treatment of patients. With the understanding that rigorous validation of AI approaches using randomized controlled studies is solicited before widespread clinical use by health authorities, the article also discusses the limitations and challenges associated with deploying AI systems to diagnose intestinal malignancies and premalignant lesions.
