AB  - Lung cancer is considered one of the leading causes of death all across the world. Various radiology-related fields increasingly have used Computer-aided diagnosis (CAD) systems. It just has already become a part of clinical work for lung cancer detection. In this article, we proposed an Adaptive Boost-based Grid Search Optimized Random Forest (Ada-GridRF) classifier that best optimized the hyperparameters of the base random forest model to identify the malignant and non-malignant nodules from the trained CT images. Improved performance speed and reduced computational complexity were the advantages of the proposed method. The proposed methodology was compared with other hyperparameter optimization techniques and also with different conventional approaches. It even outperformed the popular state-of-the-art deep learning techniques such as transfer learning and convolutional neural network. The experimental results proved that the proposed method yielded the best performance metrics of 97.97% accuracy, 100% sensitivity, 96% specificity, 96.08% precision, 98% F1-score, 4% False positives rate, and 99.8% Area under the ROC curve (AUC). It took only 8 msec to train the model. Thus, the proposed Ada-GridRF model can aid radiologists in fast lung cancer detection.
KW  - Computer-aided diagnosis; Hyperparameter optimization; Lung cancer; Grid
