AB  - Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the most common primary liver cancer and is associated with high mortality rates due to late detection and aggressive progression. Peritumoral radiomics, an emerging technique that quantitatively analyzes the tissue surrounding the tumor, has shown significant potential in enhancing the management of HCC. This paper examines the role of peritumoral radiomics in improving diagnostic accuracy, guiding personalized treatment strategies, and refining prognostic assessments. By offering unique insights into the tumor microenvironment, peritumoral radiomics enables more precise patient stratification and informs clinical decision-making. However, the integration of peritumoral radiomics into routine clinical practice faces several challenges. Addressing these challenges through continued research and innovation is crucial for the successful implementation of peritumoral radiomics in HCC management, ultimately leading to improved patient outcomes.
