AB  - Background: Computer-assisted diagnosis (CAD) is an effective approach to improve the relative survival of cancer patients, and lung nodule simulation is suitable for pulmonary nodule detection studies. Objective: To solve the problem of traditional nodule models that cannot create certain kinds of shapes of nodules in chest computed tomography (CT) images, an effective approach using modified Gaussian models is proposed and developed in this work. Methods: There are various kinds of nodules in chest CT images and their shapes can be simulated by mathematical models. However, traditional nodule models can only simulate spherical and ellipsoidal nodules. Accordingly, in this study, improved two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) Gaussian models are developed to create the various shapes of the different kinds of lung nodules. After nodule synthesis and insertion, four radiologists tried to differentiate the simulated nodules from real nodules. Results: The four types of nodules, according to their relative position in lung CT images, and the seven kinds of nodules, according to their border shape, with different size, location and density were generated by the improved 2D Gaussian model (2D-IGM) and the shapes of nodules were generated by the improved 3D Gaussian model (3D-IGM), respectively. The experimental results showed that the developed models outperformed the conventional method. Thus, the newly developed models are effective and reliable in lung nodule simulation. Conclusions: An effective approach based on modified Gaussian models is proposed and developed for lung nodule simulation in this work. The developed approach can be applied to measure nodule size, assess tumor growth rate, train machine learning algorithm in nodule detection, and other tasks.
