AB  - A novel medical diagnosis system is proposed to predict the prevalence of Coronary Artery Disease (CAD) using the inherent mathematical capabilities of Multi Criteria Decision Making (MCDM) methods. Analytic Hierarchy Processing (AHP), Technique for Order Preference by Similarity to Ideal Solution (TOPSIS) and Simple Additive Weighting (SAW) method are chosen for implementing the MCDM system. AHP is used to analyze the hierarchy structure of CAD diagnosis and determine the attribute weights based on the individual contribution of risk attributes for disease diagnosis as prescribed by an experienced cardiologist. The extended TOPSIS and SAW methods are designed for diagnosis of CAD using attribute weights obtained from AHP. The performance of the proposed system is evaluated using Cleveland heart disease dataset available online in University of California at Irvine (UCI) machine learning repository. MCDM methods provided accurate prediction results on the diagnosis of CAD similar to the medical experts.
