JO  - RADIOLOGIC CLINICS OF NORTH AMERICA
AB  - While AI applications in GI tract imaging have been less explored than in many other parts of the body, the body of published literature in the space is growing rapidly. Exciting early studies have made promising advances in the use of AI for everything from emergency radiology applications such as pneumoperitoneum and bowel obstruction, to quantification of inflammatory bowel disease, and rectal cancer staging. Even more advanced applications have gone well beyond routine clinical care, to include tasks such as predicting tumor biomarker status or even non-image interpretive tasks such as protocoling and image artifact reduction. These applications offer a glimpse of what may become routine clinical practice, but at the same time, much work is yet to be done to successfully increase the breadth and depth of applications and successfully translate them to clinical practice. To advance more rapidly, we must progress away from the currently common single-center datasets to larger, multi-institutional, and population-representative ones through better partnerships between research centers. Similarly, we need standardized data processing tools for bowel course and segmentation to accelerate data-preprocessing steps. With improved collaboration, we can realize the true potential of AI in GI imaging and herald a new era of innovation with improvement in patient care.
