AB  - In early lung cancer screening, the accurate classification of lung nodules is essential to clinical decisions and individualized treatments. Despite the considerable advances in earlier approaches, two main obstacles remain: the increasing model size that limits the clinical application with limited resource and the omission of spatial and channel-aware information that results in insufficient interpretability. To address these challenges, we propose TripleAttentionNet, a novel approach to lung nodule classification that features Triple Union Efficient Attention (TUEA) modules to automatically capture the key features of lung nodules by modeling the encoder output and desired receptive field regions, with linear complexity regarding the computation of the input sequence. We also adopt a weight sharing strategy across parallel attention pathways within the TUEA to strengthen inter-branch correlations. This not only facilitates mutual augmentation of the attention processes, but also reduces aggregate network parameters. Furthermore, we employ Neural Architecture Search (NAS) to autonomously identify the network architectures that offer a favorable balance between accuracy and parameter size. The effectiveness of the approach is confirmed by substantial experiments and extensive analyses on LIDC-IDRI, LUNA16, and LUNGx. Our model achieves state-of-the-art metrics on all the datasets. The proposed approach should benefit the field of lung cancer screening and enable better computer-aided diagnosis in resource-limited scenarios.
