AB  - Breast cancer is a great threat to woman's health. The main reason is that the detection rate of breast cancer is low, the detection results are not accurate, and the treatment of patients is not timely, so the complications and mortality of breast cancer are very high. With the upgrading of hardware and the accumulation of large-scale medical data, biological information data and medical image data are gradually huge, and the hot spots in the medical field begin to turn from hardware to data. How to dig out the important knowledge discovery about the life phenomenon from such massive medical image data has become the central problem that people care about. This article puts forward a kind .of breast ultrasound image classification method based on machine learning and diagnostic tests, through convolution trained neural network to the original data, the resulting network feature detection algorithm can effectively detect breast cancer change parts, compared with the traditional X-ray detection method, greatly improving the detection efficiency. At the same time, the pre-processing of ultrasonic images of breast cancer proves the advantages of pre-processing in breast cancer diagnosis, which can greatly increase the detection probability of breast cancer.
KW  - Breast Cancer; Ultrasound Images; Machine Learning; Convolution Neural
DO  - 10.1166/jmihi.2021.3417
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Deep Data Source Fusion with Bias-Undoing for Lung Adenocarcinoma
AU  - Feng, YL
PY  - 2020
JO  - JOURNAL OF MEDICAL IMAGING AND HEALTH INFORMATICS
AB  - Computer-aided diagnosis of early-stage lung adenocarcinoma based on deep learning is prospective for assisting the prevention and treatment of the deathly disease of lung cancer, however, relevant works face the problem of limited training data. The technique of data source fusion with the training of deep models on multiple relevant datasets is promising to resolve the lack of training data, while the bias of data distribution from different data sources exists as a universal issue to affect the learning performance. In this paper, we propose a deep learning framework based on bias-undoing data source fusion to classify early stages of lung adenocarcinoma in computed tomography (CT) images. The framework conducts learning on the integrated datasets for respectively natural image, lung nodule CT and lung adenocarcinoma CT, as designed with an organization of base parameters and bias parameters to adapt to the data distribution with bias. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed bias-undoing framework is effective to improve the performance of deep learning for lung adenocarcinoma classification, and is with great superiority to those general fusion frameworks on alleviating the effect of dataset bias.
KW  - Lung Adenocarcinoma; Computed Tomography; Deep Learning; Data Source
DO  - 10.1166/jmihi.2020.3262
ER  - 
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Artificial intelligence and machine learning in oncologic imaging
AU  - Kleesiek, J
PY  - 2020
JO  - PATHOLOGE
AB  - Machine learning (ML) is entering many areas of society, including medicine. This transformation has the potential to drastically change medicine and medical practice. These aspects become particularly clear when considering the different stages of oncologic patient care and the involved interdisciplinary and intermodality interactions. In recent publications, computers-in collaboration with humans or alone-have been outperforming humans regarding tumor identification, tumor classification, estimating prognoses, and evaluation of treatments. In addition, ML algorithms, e.g., artificial neural networks (ANNs), which constitute the drivers behind many of the latest achievements in ML, can deliver this level of performance in a reproducible, fast, and inexpensive manner. In the future, artificial intelligence applications will become an integral part of the medical profession and offer advantages for oncologic diagnostics and treatment.
KW  - Machine learning; Computer-assisted image processing; Diagnostic
DO  - 10.1007/s00292-020-00827-3
